{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788383589634,"sessionID":"ses_f9c0a6ba5ffeg327dJ3UtwgaCa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-890b05807c25d051","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"import Database from \"better-sqlite3\";\n\nexport interface BookRow {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year: number | null;\n  isbn: string | null;\n  created_at: string;\n  updated_at: string;\n}\n\nexport interface BookInput {\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year: number | null;\n  isbn: string | null;\n}\n\nconst SCHEMA = `\n  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year INTEGER,\n    isbn TEXT,\n    created_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),\n    updated_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))\n  );\n`;\n\nexport class BookStore {\n  private db: Database.Database;\n\n  constructor(dbPath: string = \":memory:\") {\n    this.db = new Database(dbPath);\n    this.db.pragma(\"journal_mode = WAL\");\n    this.db.exec(SCHEMA);\n  }\n\n  listAll(authorFilter?: string): BookRow[] {\n    if (authorFilter && authorFilter.trim().length > 0) {\n      const stmt = this.db.prepare(\n        \"SELECT * FROM books WHERE author LIKE ? COLLATE NOCASE ORDER BY id ASC\"\n      );\n      return stmt.all(`%${authorFilter}%`) as BookRow[];\n    }\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\"SELECT * FROM books ORDER BY id ASC\");\n    return stmt.all() as BookRow[];\n  }\n\n  getById(id: number): BookRow | undefined {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\");\n    return stmt.get(id) as BookRow | undefined;\n  }\n\n  create(input: BookInput): BookRow {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\n      \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\"\n    );\n    const info = stmt.run(\n      input.title,\n      input.author,\n      input.year,\n      input.isbn\n    );\n    return this.getById(Number(info.lastInsertRowid)) as BookRow;\n  }\n\n  update(id: number, input: BookInput): BookRow | undefined {\n    if (!this.getById(id)) return undefined;\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\n      `UPDATE books\n       SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ?, updated_at = datetime('now')\n       WHERE id = ?`\n    );\n    stmt.run(input.title, input.author, input.year, input.isbn, id);\n    return this.getById(id);\n  }\n\n  delete(id: number): boolean {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\");\n    const info = stmt.run(id);\n    return info.changes > 0;\n  }\n\n  close(): void {\n    this.db.close();\n  }\n}\n","filePath":"/workspace/src/db.ts"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/src/db.ts","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"src/db.ts","time":{"start":1788383589609,"end":1788383589625}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me create a separate tsconfig for tests, or I can include the tests. Actually, since I set `rootDir` to `src`, I need a separate config for tests if I want to typecheck them. Let me create a vitest config and a tsconfig that includes tests for typechecking.\n\nActually, let me keep it simple. I'll create the main source files first, then a tsconfig.test.json for the tests, or I'll just use vitest which uses its own TS handling via esbuild. For typecheck I can run `tsc --noEmit` on src only.\n\nLet me think about the structure. I'll use ESM modules (`\"type\": \"module\"`). With NodeNext module resolution, imports need `.js` extensions in the import paths within the same package. Actually, for ESM with NodeNext, we need to use `.js` extensions in import statements even when the source files are `.ts`. Wait, that's the rule for NodeNext. Let me use `.js` extensions in imports.\n\nActually, with `tsx` it handles both. And the compiled output uses `.js`. So I should use `.js` extensions in the import paths. Let me be careful about this.\n\nLet me now create the source files:\n1. `src/db.ts` - database layer with better-sqlite3\n2. `src/validation.ts` - input validation\n3. `src/routes/books.ts` - book routes\n4. `src/routes/health.ts` - health route\n5. `src/app.ts` - express app (exported for testing)\n6. `src/index.ts` - server entry point\n7. `tests/books.test.ts` - integration tests\n8. `tests/health.test.ts` - health test\n9. `tests/validation.test.ts` - unit tests for validation\n\nLet me define the Book type first. The book has: id (auto), title, author, year, isbn.\n\nFor the PUT endpoint, I need to handle updates. The validation for PUT - title and author are required (per the requirements \"title and author are required\"). For PUT, should it be a full replacement or partial update? The requirement says \"Update a book\". I'll treat PUT as a full update where title and author are required, and year and isbn are optional but if provided must be validated.\n\nActually, let me think. Typically PUT is a full replacement. I'll require title and author for PUT as well (since the requirement says \"title and author are required\"). Year and isbn can be optional or cleared. Let me make it so that on PUT, title and author are required, year and isbn are optional (can be null).\n\nLet me design the validation:\n- Create: title (required string), author (required string), year (optional, integer >= 0 or current), isbn (optional string)\n- Update: title (required string), author (required string), year (optional), isbn (optional)\n\nLet me think about what types of validation:\n- title must be a non-empty string\n- author must be a non-empty string\n- year must be an integer (if provided) and reasonable (e.g., >= 0 and <= current year + 1)\n- isbn must be a string (if provided)\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nFor the DB, I'll use better-sqlite3. The schema:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  author TEXT NOT NULL,\n  year INTEGER,\n  isbn TEXT,\n  created_at TEXT DEFAULT (datetime('now')),\n  updated_at TEXT DEFAULT (datetime('now'))\n);\n```\n\nFor the author filter on GET /books, I'll support `?author=` with a LIKE or exact match. The requirement says \"support ?author= filter\". I'll do a case-insensitive partial match or exact match. Let me do an exact (case-insensitive) match to be safe but partial might be more useful. Hmm. Let me do a case-insensitive partial match (LIKE) which is more flexible. Actually, let me re-read: \"List all books (support ?author= filter)\". I'll implement a case-insensitive exact match to be precise. Hmm, but partial is often more useful. \n\nLet me do case-insensitive partial match (LIKE %author%) since that's more flexible and commonly expected. Actually, I'll go with exact case-insensitive match to keep it clean and predictable. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. Many APIs do partial matching on filters. I'll go with case-insensitive partial match (LIKE) so \"george\" matches \"George Orwell\". That's the most useful. I'll document it.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nFor the response format, I'll return JSON. For errors, I'll return `{ error: \"message\" }`.\n\nFor HTTP status codes:\n- POST /books: 201 Created (with the created book), 400 if validation fails\n- GET /books: 200\n- GET /books/{id}: 200, 404 if not found\n- PUT /books/{id}: 200, 404 if not found, 400 if validation fails\n- DELETE /books/{id}: 204 (no content) or 200 with a message, 404 if not found\n- GET /health: 200\n\nLet me write the app. I'll structure it so that the express app is exported separately from the server start (so tests can use supertest without the server listening).\n\nLet me design:\n\n`src/db.ts`:\n```typescript\nimport Database from 'better-sqlite3';\nimport path from 'node:path';\n\nexport interface BookRow {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year: number | null;\n  isbn: string | null;\n  created_at: string;\n  updated_at: string;\n}\n\nexport interface NewBook {\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year?: number | null;\n  isbn?: string | null;\n}\n\nexport class BookStore {\n  private db: Database.Database;\n\n  constructor(dbPath?: string) {\n    this.db = new Database(dbPath ?? ':memory:');\n    this.db.pragma('journal_mode = WAL');\n    this.init();\n  }\n\n  private init() {\n    this.db.exec(`\n      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n        title TEXT NOT NULL,\n        author TEXT NOT NULL,\n        year INTEGER,\n        isbn TEXT,\n        created_at TEXT DEFAULT (datetime('now')),\n        updated_at TEXT DEFAULT (datetime('now'))\n      )\n    `);\n  }\n\n  listAll(authorFilter?: string): BookRow[] {\n    if (authorFilter && authorFilter.trim().length > 0) {\n      const stmt = this.db.prepare('SELECT * FROM books WHERE author LIKE ? COLLATE NOCASE ORDER BY id');\n      return stmt.all(`%${authorFilter}%`) as BookRow[];\n    }\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare('SELECT * FROM books ORDER BY id');\n    return stmt.all() as BookRow[];\n  }\n\n  getById(id: number): BookRow | undefined {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare('SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?');\n    return stmt.get(id) as BookRow | undefined;\n  }\n\n  create(book: NewBook): BookRow {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\n      'INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)'\n    );\n    const info = stmt.run(book.title, book.author, book.year ?? null, book.isbn ?? null);\n    const created = this.getById(Number(info.lastInsertRowid));\n    return created!;\n  }\n\n  update(id: number, book: NewBook): BookRow | undefined {\n    const existing = this.getById(id);\n    if (!existing) return undefined;\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare(\n      `UPDATE books SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ?, updated_at = datetime('now') WHERE id = ?`\n    );\n    stmt.run(book.title, book.author, book.year ?? null, book.isbn ?? null, id);\n    return this.getById(id);\n  }\n\n  delete(id: number): boolean {\n    const stmt = this.db.prepare('DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?');\n    const info = stmt.run(id);\n    return info.changes > 0;\n  }\n\n  close() {\n    this.db.close();\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, `Database` is the default export. Let me import properly. With esModuleInterop, `import Database from 'better-sqlite3'` works. The type is `Database.Database`. Let me check the types.\n\n`src/validation.ts`:\n```typescript\nexport interface ValidBookInput {\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year: number | null;\n  isbn: string | null;\n}\n\nexport function validateBook(input: unknown): { ok: true; value: ValidBookInput } | { ok: false; error: string } {\n  if (typeof input !== 'object' || input === null) {\n    return { ok: false, error: 'Request body must be a JSON object' };\n  }\n  const obj = input as Record<string, unknown>;\n  const errors: string[] = [];\n\n  if (typeof obj.title !== 'string' || obj.title.trim() === '') {\n    errors.push('title is required and must be a non-empty string');\n  }\n  if (typeof obj.author !== 'string' || obj.author.trim() === '') {\n    errors.push('author is required and must be a non-empty string');\n  }\n\n  let year: number | null = null;\n  if (obj.year !== undefined && obj.year !== null) {\n    if (typeof obj.year !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(obj.year)) {\n      errors.push('year must be an integer');\n    } else if (obj.year < 0 || obj.year > new Date().getFullYear() + 1) {\n      errors.push('year must be between 0 and ' + (new Date().getFullYear() + 1));\n    } else {\n      year = obj.year;\n    }\n  }\n\n  let isbn: string | null = null;\n  if (obj.isbn !== undefined && obj.isbn !== null) {\n    if (typeof obj.isbn !== 'string') {\n      errors.push('isbn must be a string');\n    } else {\n      isbn = obj.isbn.trim() === '' ? null : obj.isbn;\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (errors.length > 0) {\n    return { ok: false, error: errors.join('; ') };\n  }\n\n  const title = (obj.title as string).trim();\n  const author = (obj.author as string).trim();\n  return { ok: true, value: { title, author, year, isbn } };\n}\n```\n\nWait, the validation result union is awkward for TS. Let me design it differently so tests can check. Let me make a result type with discriminated union properly typed.\n\nActually let me reconsider the structure to make it cleaner. I'll have:\n```typescript\nexport type ValidationResult =\n  | { valid: true; value: ValidBookInput }\n  | { valid: false; errors: string[] };\n```\n\nThis is a discriminated union. Good.\n\n`src/app.ts`:\n```typescript\nimport express, { Request, Response } from 'express';\nimport { BookStore } from './db.js';\nimport { validateBook } from './validation.js';\n\nexport interface AppDeps {\n  store: BookStore;\n}\n\nexport function createApp(deps: AppDeps) {\n  const app = express();\n  app.use(express.json());\n\n  app.get('/health', (_req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    res.status(200).json({ status: 'ok' });\n  });\n\n  app.get('/books', (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const author = typeof req.query.author === 'string' ? req.query.author : undefined;\n    const books = deps.store.listAll(author);\n    res.json(books);\n  });\n\n  app.get('/books/:id', (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const id = Number(req.params.id);\n    if (!Number.isInteger(id)) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'id must be an integer' });\n    }\n    const book = deps.store.getById(id);\n    if (!book) {\n      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'book not found' });\n    }\n    res.json(book);\n  });\n\n  app.post('/books', (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const result = validateBook(req.body);\n    if (!result.valid) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: result.errors.join('; ') });\n    }\n    const book = deps.store.create(result.value);\n    res.status(201).json(book);\n  });\n\n  app.put('/books/:id', (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const id = Number(req.params.id);\n    if (!Number.isInteger(id)) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'id must be an integer' });\n    }\n    const result = validateBook(req.body);\n    if (!result.valid) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: result.errors.join('; ') });\n    }\n    const book = deps.store.update(id, result.value);\n    if (!book) {\n      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'book not found' });\n    }\n    res.json(book);\n  });\n\n  app.delete('/books/:id', (req: Request, res: Response) => {\n    const id = Number(req.params.id);\n    if (!Number.isInteger(id)) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'id must be an integer' });\n    }\n    const deleted = deps.store.delete(id);\n    if (!deleted) {\n      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'book not found' });\n    }\n    res.status(204).send();\n  });\n\n  return app;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I need to be careful about the JSON body parsing error handling. If a malformed JSON is sent, express.json() will throw a 400 error by default? Actually it passes the error to the error handler. Let me add an error handler.\n\nAlso, for `Number(req.params.id)`, if the id is like \"abc\", Number returns NaN. Number.isInteger(NaN) is false. Good. If id is \"1.5\", Number returns 1.5, not integer, so 400. Good. If \"1\", returns 1, integer. Good.\n\n`src/index.ts`:\n```typescript\nimport { createApp } from './app.js';\nimport { BookStore } from './db.js';\n\nconst dbPath = process.env.DB_PATH ?? 'books.db';\nconst store = new BookStore(dbPath);\nconst app = createApp({ store });\n\nconst port = process.env.PORT ? Number(process.env.PORT) : 3000;\nconst server = app.listen(port, () => {\n  console.log(`Book collection API listening on http://localhost:${port}`);\n});\n\nconst shutdown = () => {\n  server.close(() => {\n    store.close();\n    process.exit(0);\n  });\n};\nprocess.on('SIGINT', shutdown);\nprocess.on('SIGTERM', shutdown);\n```\n\nFor the tests, I'll use in-memory database. Let me create tests using vitest + supertest.\n\n`tests/books.test.ts`:\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect, beforeEach } from 'vitest';\nimport request from 'supertest';\nimport { createApp } from '../src/app.js';\nimport { BookStore } from '../src/db.js';\n\ndescribe('Books API', () => {\n  let store: BookStore;\n  let app: ReturnType<typeof createApp>;\n\n  beforeEach(() => {\n    store = new BookStore(':memory:');\n    app = createApp({ store });\n  });\n\n  it('creates a book and retrieves it', async () => {\n    const res = await request(app)\n      .post('/books')\n      .send({ title: '1984', author: 'George Orwell', year: 1949, isbn: '978-0451524935' });\n    expect(res.status).toBe(201);\n    expect(res.body.id).toBeDefined();\n    expect(res.body.title).toBe('1984');\n    ...\n  });\n  ...\n});\n```\n\nLet me write the tests. I'll write more than 3 tests to be thorough.\n\nLet me also handle the malformed JSON. I'll add an error handler in app.ts.\n\nLet me write all the files now. Let me be careful with the import extensions - with NodeNext and ESM, imports need `.js` extension. But with tsx/vitest (esbuild), it also resolves. The compiled JS in dist/ uses `.js`. So I need `.js` in imports.\n\nActually wait - for vitest, when importing `../src/app.js`, vitest (via vite/esbuild) will resolve the `.ts` file. Vite has a resolver that handles `.js` -> `.ts`. Yes, vitest supports this. Good.\n\nLet me write everything now.\n\nOne concern: better-sqlite3 is a native module. It needs to be compiled. The prebuild-install should have downloaded a prebuilt binary. Let me verify it loads.\n\nLet me write the files and then test.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063f7cf89001iq61q55eYN3Mnp","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a6ba5ffeg327dJ3UtwgaCa","messageID":"msg_063f69621001OCoWog2qEL4wEj"}}
